1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:13,099
This is the centre millwright. Where the millwright serves one lab, this one serves the whole centre: cross-lab projects, permissions on shared servers, and the loop that finds drift.

2
00:00:13,500 --> 00:00:22,800
Its file says the job is centre-wide reconciliation: per-project permissions on shared servers, cross-lab project provisioning with guest invites, and membership-drift detection.

3
00:00:22,800 --> 00:00:37,700
Reconciling drift is its primary job: compare what the project records say with what Slack, GitHub and the filesystem actually hold. One project at a time, so a bad step cannot cascade.

4
00:00:38,100 --> 00:00:47,400
It does not onboard individual members; that is the lab millwright's. It never modifies a lab's records repository; it only reads them.

5
00:00:47,400 --> 00:00:59,100
It does not act on shared infrastructure without registrar sign-off. And it never deletes a Slack channel: it archives, so the registrar can restore.

6
00:00:59,500 --> 00:01:07,599
A real run, dry-run only, on a laptop with no centre record. The mayor asks for the drift loop.

7
00:01:07,599 --> 00:01:20,500
The verdict is Drift. It says plainly which comparison it could not make here, lists each item of local drift with what it would do, and applied nothing.

8
00:01:20,900 --> 00:01:36,599
To use it, type to Claude Code and name it: ask the centre millwright to reconcile a project, or to run the drift loop. It runs on the registrar's machine and shows the diff before anything is applied.

9
00:01:36,599 --> 00:01:49,199
The first line is the verdict: Reconciled, Drift or Blocked. Then each delta, what it would change, and the confirmation it is waiting for.
